<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Josephus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">37–c. 100</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Flavius Josephus, Titus Flavius Josephus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flavius Josephus was a Jewish historian and a former commander in the revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> in 67 CE, he won the favour of Vespasian and wrote in Rome The Jewish War and Jewish Antiquities, histories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from the creation to the revolt of 66–73. His works are the main source for Herodian Judea and its rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josephus.jpg" alt="Josephus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scan by NYPL · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph,_son_of_Gorian,_called_Flavius_Josephus_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
